From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0239] clock: the demand C2-03 opened is handed back, not dropped

C2-03 ends with the gate branch running and a demand the ledger still holds:
the token is acquired, completed as running, and deliberately never released,
because there was no verified way to stop the clock and the card existed to
prove the enable, not to undo it. That leaves the port in a state only the
device can defend: a backend that keeps a clock on forever is not a backend
that owns it. C2-04 is that release.

The disable is not a second store with a nicer comment. Three things have to
hold before the ledger is told anything, and each of them is a way the card can
refuse:

  * The demand is still this driver's. The token from gateClocks() is kept in
    fGateToken/fGateTokenHeld and cleared the moment it is released, so a
    disable can never hand back a demand it does not own, a second disable in
    the same boot has nothing to retract, and a backend that was never gated
    reports no-token instead of guessing.

  * The branch is the enable this driver left behind. The CBCR is read first
    (phase=disable-request, enable/off/value printed), and a branch that is
    already halted or that does not carry the enable bit is refused with
    already-halted / not-enabled and zero writes. Storing into either state
    would be a row claiming a transition that never happened.

  * The store withdraws exactly one bit. saved & ~kGccBranchEnable keeps the
    divider fields and the halt status the hardware owns; the vendor driver's
    regmap_update_bits(BIT(0)) is the same operation written differently.

The verdict is the hardware's, not the row's: CLK_OFF has to rise within the
same 2500x10us budget the enable waits for it to fall, using the same bounded
poll shape as gateClocks(). Only then does the ledger get ledgerRelease(token,
true) - stopped=true, so the demand it was owed becomes a confirmation instead
of an IOError "hardware-still-on". The success row prints hold=0 and
last-holder=1, which is what makes the handover visible in a single line.

Two failures keep everything where it was, and that is the point of the card:

  * A halt timeout leaves the demand and the branch alone. Undoing the store to
    make the ring look clean would be exactly the unverified write this card
    exists to rule out; the row says hold=1 detail=halt-timeout, the token is
    still held, and start() reports detail=disable-incomplete while staying
    Ready.

  * A neighbour that moved after the halt is treated the same way. The halt
    happened, but this driver cannot account for what else the store touched,
    so it reports neighbour-changed, keeps the demand, and does not re-enable.

Success ends by running the C2-03 store again. Recovery is not politeness: the
C2-08 counter's third source is the gated branch, and the boot must end in the
state the C2-03 baseline leaves - branch running, demand held by the same
driver, same reads for everything that follows - or a disable candidate would
silently change every measurement after it. The harness pins that cost: the
gate pass is 29 reads, the disable adds one request plus the halt poll, and the
recovery re-runs the whole pass, so a gate+disable boot is reads=49 writes=3
(the enable, the retraction and the recovery enable).

The host fixture learned the hardware behaviour the ring depends on. A branch
CBCR's halt status follows its own enable bit: a running branch reports
CLK_OFF=0, a disabled one reports CLK_OFF=1. The fixture's plain register file
could not express that - the store writes the value it read, which never
carries the hardware's bit - so a disabled branch kept reporting "running" and
the poll burned all 2500 tries. The model is opt-in (gccHaltFollowsEnable), the
never-halts case tracks the store that withdrew the enable (gccDisableStored)
so it cannot fire on the first read of a run, and the plain file stays the
input for the two refusal arms: 0x0 is not-enabled, and a device whose status
follows the enable reports already-halted for the same handover.

The ADT side keeps the two properties dependent: --clock-disable implies
--clock-gate (and therefore --gcc), because a tree that asks for a disable
without the enable is a tree validateProperties refuses, and the generator will
not emit one. The default, provider, diagnostic, measurement and gate trees
keep their audited digests; the new property is additive, exactly removable,
and the combined tree is the gate tree plus one 4-byte property.

Nothing here is reachable from an image that does not carry sweet,clock-disable
in its device tree, so the zero-write default and the C2-03 ring are unchanged.

